So when `iter_route_contexts` is called and a route is `_IncludedRouter`, it creates a `RouteContext(original_route, route_context)`. The `original_route` is the `APIRoute` and `route_context` is the `_EffectiveRouteContext`.

Looking at `RouteContext.__getattr__`:
```python
def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:
    return getattr(self._effective_route, name)
```

And `_effective_route` property:
```python
@property
def _effective_route(self) -> BaseRoute | _EffectiveRouteContext:
    if self._route_context is not None:
        return self._route_context
    return self.route
```

So when `_route_context` is not None, `__getattr__` delegates to the `_EffectiveRouteContext`. But `_EffectiveRouteContext` doesn't have `deprecated_at` declared as an attribute! So accessing `context.deprecated_at` will raise `AttributeError`.

